WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:03.303
-Our first guest tonight
is an Emmy-nominated actress,

00:00:03.303 --> 00:00:05.171
musician, and comedian
you know from films

00:00:05.171 --> 00:00:07.373
such as "Ocean's Eight,"
"Crazy Rich Asians,"

00:00:07.373 --> 00:00:09.708
and "The Farewell."
Her new movie, "Quiz Lady,"

00:00:09.708 --> 00:00:11.911
is streaming now on Hulu.
Let's take a look.

00:00:11.911 --> 00:00:14.547
-And your time starts now.

00:00:14.547 --> 00:00:16.816
What is the square root of 196?
-14.

00:00:16.816 --> 00:00:17.884 line:60%
-Ron.
-14.

00:00:17.884 --> 00:00:19.051 line:60%
-Correct.
How many hearts

00:00:19.051 --> 00:00:20.653
does an octopus have?
-Three.

00:00:20.653 --> 00:00:21.787
-Ron.
-Three.

00:00:21.787 --> 00:00:23.123
-Correct.
-What?

00:00:23.123 --> 00:00:25.758
-What spice is derived
from Crocus sativus?

00:00:25.758 --> 00:00:27.460
-Saffron.
-Feel like an answer hog.

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:29.762
Uh, saffron.
-Correct.

00:00:29.762 --> 00:00:30.997
-Carbon dioxide.
-Very good.

00:00:30.997 --> 00:00:32.365
-Wellington, New Zealand.
-Correct.

00:00:32.365 --> 00:00:34.333
-Saturn.
"Our American Cousin."

00:00:34.333 --> 00:00:36.536
The equal sign.
-Harry Potter.

00:00:36.536 --> 00:00:37.770
-"The Hobbit."
-Correct.

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:39.105
-Sorry.
[ Dog sneezes ]

00:00:39.105 --> 00:00:40.473
Bless you.
-Ron...

00:00:40.473 --> 00:00:43.342
-Please welcome back to the show
Awkwafina, everybody!

00:00:43.342 --> 00:00:45.344
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:45.344 --> 00:00:48.381
[ Up-tempo music plays ]

00:00:48.381 --> 00:00:58.525
♪♪

00:00:58.525 --> 00:01:01.093
Welcome back!
-Hey. Good to be back.

00:01:01.093 --> 00:01:03.596
-It's great to have you.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:03.596 --> 00:01:08.200
I really appreciate anytime
someone flies in from London

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:09.635
and still makes time
for our show.

00:01:09.635 --> 00:01:10.903
You did that this morning.
-I did.

00:01:10.903 --> 00:01:13.006
-How are you feeling?
-I feel -- I feel good.

00:01:13.006 --> 00:01:15.208
I feel -- I feel refreshed,
I guess.

00:01:15.208 --> 00:01:17.076
-How was the flight?
-The flight was good.

00:01:17.076 --> 00:01:20.046
I slept a little bit.
I had been coming back

00:01:20.046 --> 00:01:22.282
from a kind of
a weird London moment,

00:01:22.282 --> 00:01:23.616
though, so I just...
-Okay.

00:01:23.616 --> 00:01:24.917
-Yeah.
-Happy to be home?

00:01:24.917 --> 00:01:26.386
-Happy to be home, yeah.
-Okay, gotcha.

00:01:26.386 --> 00:01:28.521
-"Weird London moment" --
that's very cryptic.

00:01:28.521 --> 00:01:32.058
-Yeah, I -- I had
a chocolate moment.

00:01:32.058 --> 00:01:33.693
-What was a chocolate moment?

00:01:33.693 --> 00:01:35.828
-That's what people ask me --
right? -- in London.

00:01:35.828 --> 00:01:38.431
So, I was at this lovely hotel
that I stay at

00:01:38.431 --> 00:01:41.066
when I go there.
Love them.

00:01:41.066 --> 00:01:44.537
And I guess I woke up one night
completely ravenous.

00:01:44.537 --> 00:01:47.173
And you know, like, in London,
they love

00:01:47.173 --> 00:01:49.309
leaving little chocolates.
They leave little chocolates.

00:01:49.309 --> 00:01:51.344
-A nice hotel is like, "We're
gonna leave some chocolate."

00:01:51.344 --> 00:01:53.479
-Totally, totally.
And I was just --

00:01:53.479 --> 00:01:55.415
I just -
I guess I just hogged

00:01:55.415 --> 00:01:57.283
all these chocolates
in the middle of the night.

00:01:57.283 --> 00:01:58.951
-Like in a fugue state.
You have no memory of it.

00:01:58.951 --> 00:02:02.021
-It was in the dark, I think.
It was -- I was

00:02:02.021 --> 00:02:03.322
not cognizant
of what was going on.

00:02:03.322 --> 00:02:08.127
When I woke up, there was
chocolate all over the bed.

00:02:08.127 --> 00:02:10.730
Not only -- On the walls.
-Yeah.

00:02:10.730 --> 00:02:14.000
-On the door handles
in the bathroom, right?

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:16.202
-Yeah.
-So I felt horrified

00:02:16.202 --> 00:02:18.003
because it's also, like,
I didn't want them to --

00:02:18.003 --> 00:02:20.739
you know, they know.
And so I cleaned up

00:02:20.739 --> 00:02:22.942
what I could
and I took the wrappers out,

00:02:22.942 --> 00:02:25.912
and then I put them
by each of the chocolate stains.

00:02:25.912 --> 00:02:28.281
[ Laughter ]

00:02:28.281 --> 00:02:29.715
-Yeah, yeah, yeah, so then
nobody had to wonder.

00:02:29.715 --> 00:02:32.118
-Yeah, you know what that is.
It's chocolate.

00:02:32.118 --> 00:02:33.853
-Housekeeping could be like,
"It's bad,

00:02:33.853 --> 00:02:36.555
but it could be worse."
-"I think it's chocolate."

00:02:36.555 --> 00:02:38.257
-Yeah, yeah.
-Yes, yes.

00:02:38.257 --> 00:02:40.493
-Of course, the best
would be if it, like --

00:02:40.493 --> 00:02:42.594
if it was not chocolate,

00:02:42.594 --> 00:02:45.131
but then you just still left
the chocolate wrappers.

00:02:45.131 --> 00:02:47.166
[ Laughter ]

00:02:47.166 --> 00:02:49.935
Like, that might be my move
in the future.

00:02:49.935 --> 00:02:51.437
-I don't know if that
would be, like, the best.

00:02:51.437 --> 00:02:52.971
You know what I mean?
-I think it's the best.

00:02:52.971 --> 00:02:54.507
-Let's agree to disagree.

00:02:54.507 --> 00:02:56.509
-We'll agree to disagree
on that one, yeah.

00:02:56.509 --> 00:03:00.346
-This is a wonderful movie
with you and Sandra Oh.

00:03:00.346 --> 00:03:02.015
You play sisters.
It's very sweet.

00:03:02.015 --> 00:03:04.350
It's very funny.
You play the more rigid

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:06.518
of two sisters,
who is sort of obsessed,

00:03:06.518 --> 00:03:08.554
as we maybe could guess
from that clip,

00:03:08.554 --> 00:03:09.989
with game shows.
Were you someone,

00:03:09.989 --> 00:03:12.158
as a kid who watched
a lot of game shows?

00:03:12.158 --> 00:03:14.126
-Yeah, I definitely did.

00:03:14.126 --> 00:03:15.661
There is something
very comforting

00:03:15.661 --> 00:03:18.297
about their kind of regularity.

00:03:18.297 --> 00:03:21.133
Every day, it happens,
you know, at a certain time.

00:03:21.133 --> 00:03:24.870
I really liked, like,
other ones, like, you know,

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:26.505
"Fear Factor"
and stuff like that.

00:03:26.505 --> 00:03:27.706
That's not necessarily
a game show.

00:03:27.706 --> 00:03:30.076
-There's an element of game
to it.

00:03:30.076 --> 00:03:32.211
It's weird watching that --
Or my parents,

00:03:32.211 --> 00:03:33.713
who still watch "Jeopardy!"
every night.

00:03:33.713 --> 00:03:35.314
Like, there is something
to watching a game

00:03:35.314 --> 00:03:37.449
that is about trivia
and knowledge.

00:03:37.449 --> 00:03:38.651
-Yes.
-I remember,

00:03:38.651 --> 00:03:40.486
when we were a kid,
how often we'd watch --

00:03:40.486 --> 00:03:42.688
I don't know,
maybe it's before your time --

00:03:42.688 --> 00:03:44.156
There's a game called
"Press Your Luck,"

00:03:44.156 --> 00:03:46.793
which is just the dumbest game.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:46.793 --> 00:03:48.661
Like, you don't get better.

00:03:48.661 --> 00:03:51.530
The only thing people do is
randomly, like, press a button,

00:03:51.530 --> 00:03:53.899
and it just stops somewhere.
And then you're like,

00:03:53.899 --> 00:03:55.468
"Well, you pressed it,
and that's your luck."

00:03:55.468 --> 00:03:57.170
-Yeah.
-And I just, like --

00:03:57.170 --> 00:03:59.272
every day, I would watch it,
and I'd feel like

00:03:59.272 --> 00:04:01.174
I could be just
such a better person now.

00:04:01.174 --> 00:04:02.775
-Yeah, did you...
[ Laughter ]

00:04:02.775 --> 00:04:04.477
Was there, like,
an algorithm to it?

00:04:04.477 --> 00:04:07.646
-No. And I think
it was, like, before computers.

00:04:07.646 --> 00:04:09.649
-Wow.
-So I think you pressed it,

00:04:09.649 --> 00:04:12.050
and a guy behind the screen
would just be like,

00:04:12.050 --> 00:04:13.553
"I don't know,
put the light on for this one."

00:04:13.553 --> 00:04:15.387
[ Laughter ]

00:04:15.387 --> 00:04:17.657
I think it was pretty shady,
what was going on.

00:04:17.657 --> 00:04:19.158
-That does sound shady.

00:04:19.158 --> 00:04:20.926
-You got to work
with an incredible cast.

00:04:20.926 --> 00:04:22.728
We saw a standard clip.
We saw Jason Schwartzman,

00:04:22.728 --> 00:04:25.431
we saw Will Ferrell in the clip,
Holland Taylor.

00:04:25.431 --> 00:04:26.899
Was this a fun movie to make?

00:04:26.899 --> 00:04:28.968
-It was such
a fun movie to make.

00:04:28.968 --> 00:04:31.804
It was definitely, like,
a collaborative effort.

00:04:31.804 --> 00:04:33.706
When all those actors
kind of signed on,

00:04:33.706 --> 00:04:35.307
I couldn't believe that.

00:04:35.307 --> 00:04:38.177
Like, they were kind of
like the dream, like, people

00:04:38.177 --> 00:04:39.312
that we wanted for those roles,

00:04:39.312 --> 00:04:41.180
and we didn't know
that they would be down.

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:43.215
And then they were.
And obviously Will Ferrell is

00:04:43.215 --> 00:04:47.286
incredible as a game show host.
-It is really fun to watch.

00:04:47.286 --> 00:04:49.421
I mean, not only
on "Celebrity Jeopardy!"

00:04:49.421 --> 00:04:52.358
but I bet he played at
like seven other game show hosts

00:04:52.358 --> 00:04:55.360
in his time at "SNL,"
so it was very fun to see again.

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:56.429
-Yeah. Very, very fun.

00:04:56.429 --> 00:04:59.265
-You also had a costar,
which was a pug.

00:04:59.265 --> 00:05:00.800
There was a pug in the movie.
-There was.

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:03.302
-And there's your pug costar.
-Yeah.

00:05:03.302 --> 00:05:04.770
-And what's
the pug actor's name?

00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:08.007
-Oh, my God. Oh, no.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:08.007 --> 00:05:09.408
-I have it
if you don't remember.

00:05:09.408 --> 00:05:11.209
This is so embarrassing
because they are here.

00:05:11.209 --> 00:05:14.180
They wanted to say hello.
-No, no. Oh, no, they're not.

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:17.349
I wouldn't remember their names.

00:05:17.349 --> 00:05:19.651
-Look, I'm not going to pull
some Ellen move,

00:05:19.651 --> 00:05:21.420
where the -- "Oh, my God.
Do you know what?"

00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:22.788
[ Laughter ]

00:05:22.788 --> 00:05:23.922
-Are they gonna...
-Do you know

00:05:23.922 --> 00:05:26.058
what Awkwafina just did?
You just waved at Wally.

00:05:26.058 --> 00:05:29.329
-Yeah. Wally.
-Can we please stop doing this?

00:05:29.329 --> 00:05:31.363
-I'm sorry, I love --
but I love Wally.

00:05:31.363 --> 00:05:33.232
-You know Wally from "SNL."
-Yes.

00:05:33.232 --> 00:05:34.600
-Do you want me to come up?
Should I come up?

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:36.602
-No.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:36.602 --> 00:05:39.672
-Why not?
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:39.672 --> 00:05:41.874
I'll allow you --

00:05:41.874 --> 00:05:44.076
I'll allow you
to walk through once,

00:05:44.076 --> 00:05:46.078
pumping your fist
like you're really famous.

00:05:46.078 --> 00:05:47.713
Go ahead.
-Alright?

00:05:47.713 --> 00:05:49.782
-Yeah!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:49.782 --> 00:05:52.552
-Whoo!

00:05:52.552 --> 00:05:55.454
-Wally's the dude, man.
-Oh, my God.

00:05:55.454 --> 00:05:56.755
You know what's happening
right now?

00:05:56.755 --> 00:05:59.125
This dog is like, "Oh,
she remembers Wally's name."

00:05:59.125 --> 00:06:01.060
[ Laughter ]

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:02.795
"Oh, Wally.

00:06:02.795 --> 00:06:04.830
I did multiple scenes
with her,

00:06:04.830 --> 00:06:06.732
but Wally...
-"I was her costar."

00:06:06.732 --> 00:06:08.501
-...holds her cue cards
once on 'SNL,'

00:06:08.501 --> 00:06:10.803
and now she's like..."
Uh, Linguine.

00:06:10.803 --> 00:06:13.172
-Linguine was his name
in the movie, yes.

00:06:13.172 --> 00:06:14.573
-Oh, in the movie.
-So I don't even know

00:06:14.573 --> 00:06:17.109
the name of the dog.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:17.109 --> 00:06:18.844
Well, you know what?
Let's come up

00:06:18.844 --> 00:06:20.212
with a name for him.
What do you think?

00:06:20.212 --> 00:06:22.348
-He has a real name.
I feel -- I just --

00:06:22.348 --> 00:06:24.750
I feel like it was, like. Caleb.
-Uh-huh.

00:06:24.750 --> 00:06:27.252
I'll go with Caleb.
-Yeah.

00:06:27.252 --> 00:06:29.621
Was it -- How many --
Because sometimes in movies,

00:06:29.621 --> 00:06:31.423
they have to, like,
rotate in the pugs.

00:06:31.423 --> 00:06:32.825
Was it more than one?
-They do.

00:06:32.825 --> 00:06:34.960
There was more than one.
-Okay. Gotcha.

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:37.430
Well, I mean, it was --
I think it was a good job

00:06:37.430 --> 00:06:39.865
for this specific pug.
He was a bit older.

00:06:39.865 --> 00:06:42.467
-Yeah, and because, I think,
as an actor,

00:06:42.467 --> 00:06:44.170
obviously you knew
it wasn't going to be long-term.

00:06:44.170 --> 00:06:45.738
And so you didn't want
to get too close to them

00:06:45.738 --> 00:06:48.107
by, like, learning their name.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:48.107 --> 00:06:50.976
-Um, uh, no, no, no.

00:06:50.976 --> 00:06:53.445
-I don't care that you don't
know these dog's names.

00:06:53.445 --> 00:06:55.514
By the way,
neither do these dogs.

00:06:55.514 --> 00:06:57.916
-I forget his middle name.
That's true.

00:06:57.916 --> 00:07:00.052
No, but, no, he was
a pleasure to work with.

00:07:00.052 --> 00:07:03.656
He did have a stunt double
that was female.

00:07:03.656 --> 00:07:06.859
And, like, a lot --
significantly younger.

00:07:06.859 --> 00:07:10.295
-That might be worse
than the pay gap

00:07:10.295 --> 00:07:13.399
is that one -- the male pug
gets to, like, be the actor.

00:07:13.399 --> 00:07:14.866
[ Laughter ]

00:07:14.866 --> 00:07:16.435
And then, when there's a stunt,
they're like,

00:07:16.435 --> 00:07:20.806
"Get the young-woman pug
for the dangerous stuff."

00:07:20.806 --> 00:07:22.174
[ Laughter ]

00:07:22.174 --> 00:07:24.209
-It actually -- She wasn't
even doing stunts for him.

00:07:24.209 --> 00:07:26.278
She would just -- you know,
she would relieve him

00:07:26.278 --> 00:07:28.680
when he needed a break.
-Well, that's very, very sweet.

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:30.415
-It was nice, yeah.

00:07:30.415 --> 00:07:32.218
-I also want to ask --
the last time you were here --

00:07:32.218 --> 00:07:34.754
and it has been five years,
so thank you for being back --

00:07:34.754 --> 00:07:36.088
It's so lovely to see you.
-Thanks for having me.

00:07:36.088 --> 00:07:37.523
-We talked about
how your grandmother --

00:07:37.523 --> 00:07:39.291
her review
of "Crazy Rich Asians"

00:07:39.291 --> 00:07:42.028
was, she did not like your wig.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:42.028 --> 00:07:44.363
-And I was like.
"Any other thoughts, Grandma?"

00:07:44.363 --> 00:07:47.033
-So, my question is,
has she seen this one?

00:07:47.033 --> 00:07:52.271
-I feel like someone
had put it on for her,

00:07:52.271 --> 00:07:56.576
and then -- ad she, like,
in totally normal fashion,

00:07:56.576 --> 00:07:58.777
just kind of paid attention,
then, like, left.

00:07:58.777 --> 00:08:00.813
And then I don't understand it.
But then is also

00:08:00.813 --> 00:08:03.583
glued to her iPad
like 24 hours a day,

00:08:03.583 --> 00:08:05.751
like watching TVs and movies,
you know?

00:08:05.751 --> 00:08:08.053
So it's like,
when it comes to my movies,

00:08:08.053 --> 00:08:09.422
I don't know if she's like,
"I don't get it.

00:08:09.422 --> 00:08:10.789
I don't like it," you know?
-Gotcha.

00:08:10.789 --> 00:08:12.091
-But I think that --
-Did she feel

00:08:12.091 --> 00:08:13.993
like there's -- like,
if she described your work,

00:08:13.993 --> 00:08:16.061
does she feel like it lacks --
Does she feel like

00:08:16.061 --> 00:08:18.531
it's not different enough
from film to film?

00:08:18.531 --> 00:08:19.699
Like, what do you think
her criticism is?

00:08:19.699 --> 00:08:21.734
-I don't know, I don't know,

00:08:21.734 --> 00:08:24.436
I feel like it's just being like
an Asian grandma a little bit.

00:08:24.436 --> 00:08:26.271
It's like they're never
really going to tell you,

00:08:26.271 --> 00:08:27.973
like, when you kill it,
you know?

00:08:27.973 --> 00:08:30.242
-Are they just afraid that,
like, if they praise you,

00:08:30.242 --> 00:08:34.313
they'll disappear?
[ Laughter ]

00:08:34.313 --> 00:08:36.515
-I think -- I think so.

00:08:36.515 --> 00:08:38.183
Something in
the realm of that, yeah.

00:08:38.183 --> 00:08:39.385
-Something like that.

00:08:39.385 --> 00:08:42.187
Well, I have no problem here,
praising you.

00:08:42.187 --> 00:08:44.356
It's so lovely to have you back.
It's just such a joy to see you.

00:08:44.356 --> 00:08:45.958
Thank you so much.
-Thank you so much.

00:08:45.958 --> 00:08:47.126
-That's Awkwafina, everyone.

00:08:47.126 --> 00:08:49.528
"Quiz Ladies"
is streaming now on Hulu.

00:08:49.528 --> 00:08:51.430
And that's Wally
with the cue card.

00:08:51.430 --> 00:08:53.900
We'll be right back
with Micaela Diamond.

